        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PULL QUOTE -->
  <section class="section quote">
    <div class="wrap">
      <blockquote class="pull rv">Because the alternative to held is not resolution. It is <em>violence or collapse</em>.</blockquote>
      <p class="attr rv d1">From the artist statement</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE ASK -->
  <section class="section ask">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow on-dark rv" style="justify-content:center; display:inline-flex">§ 03 · The ask</span>
      <p class="big rv d1" style="margin-top:clamp(1.4rem,3vw,2.4rem)">Hold all of it.<span class="b">And keep standing.</span></p>
      <p class="tail rv d2">The show offers no resolution. It asks one thing of you.</p>
      <p class="standout rv d3">That you can is the <em>most hopeful thing</em> it knows.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- STATEMENT -->
  <section class="section statement-sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <details class="statement">
        <summary>
          <span class="lbl">Artist <em>statement</em></span>
          <span class="open-pill">Read <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">→</span></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="stmt-body">
          <div class="stmt-prose">
            <p class="open">I went looking for God and found a list instead. It was not a dignified list.</p>
            <p>When my marriage ended and I was flat on my back in a desert, unable to stand, I did not call on the God I believe in. I called on everyone. Krishna. Jesus. Hanuman. Donald Duck. My own rage. Whatever was within reach.</p>
            <p>That should embarrass me. It doesn't anymore, and that is the hopeful part.</p>
            <p>Because here is what I learned face down in the dust. You are not who you believe you are. You are who you reach for when you are breaking<sup class="fn-marker" data-fn="fn1" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Open footnote one">*</sup>. And that crowd you reach for is a circus. Gods and cartoons. Saints and villains. The Bhagavad Gita and a video game. The holy ones you would name in public, and the ones you would never admit to, the rage and the revenge and the denial, sitting right there in the same hand.</p>
            <p>I painted all of them. With the same love. Because that is honestly how we use them<sup class="fn-marker" data-fn="fn2" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Open footnote two">**</sup>.</p>
            <p>And you do it too. Everyone alive right now is calling on something to get through this century, the whole species reaching at once for the most ridiculous and magnificent collection of gods imaginable. You are not broken for it. You are gloriously, hilariously human, in a time that gives no one solid ground.</p>
            <p>So the painting does not ask you to clean up your list. You can't, and you shouldn't. It does not ask you to resolve yourself into one tidy person. There is no such person, and they would be unbearable company. It asks one thing. Hold it. The whole contradictory, sacred, embarrassing pile. The god and the devil and the duck. And keep standing.</p>
            <p>That is the woman on the broken ground. She is not free. She is not winning. She is holding everything that will not reconcile, with what I am fairly sure is a small smile, and she has not fallen.</p>
            <p>Neither have you. That is great news. Because the alternative to held is not resolution. It is violence or collapse.</p>
            <span class="sign">Raghava KK · Bangalore · 2026</span>
          </div>
          <div class="fnotes">
            <p class="fnh">Two notes, expand to read</p>
            <details class="fnote" id="fn1">
              <summary><span class="star">*</span><span>You are who you reach for when you are breaking</span><span class="ch">Open</span></summary>
              <div class="fb">
                <p>Plainly: in normal life you can curate yourself. You choose your words, your values, the image you present. So who you "are" looks like a decision you make. But when something breaks you, a death, a divorce, a war, real fear, you don't have time to curate. You reach. Automatically. For whatever actually steadies you. And that reflex shows what you genuinely run on, not what you claim to.</p>
                <p>A few plain examples. Someone says they're rational and secular, then their kid gets sick and they find themselves praying. Someone says they forgive, then they get hurt and reach straight for revenge. Someone preaches calm, then panics and reaches for control. The crisis strips the performance and shows the actual operating system underneath. When you were breaking, which of these did you actually grab? <em>That one is you.</em></p>
              </div>
            </details>
            <details class="fnote" id="fn2">
              <summary><span class="star">**</span><span>Because that is honestly how we use them</span><span class="ch">Open</span></summary>
              <div class="fb">
                <p>It means the love in the painting is not Raghava approving of these gods. It is him being accurate about how we actually treat them.</p>
                <p>In real life we don't keep our ugly impulses at arm's length. We don't reach for revenge reluctantly and Krishna warmly. We reach for both the same way: with full intensity, total intimacy, complete dependence. When someone wrongs you, the fantasy of revenge is not cold and clinical. It is comforting. You curl up in it. You feed it. You return to it like a shrine. That is devotion. Same with denial, with rage, with ego. We treat them like gods because that is functionally what they are to us, things we turn to, trust, and let run our lives in the bad moments.</p>
                <p>So if Raghava painted the noble gods with love and the dark ones with disgust, he would be lying. The honesty is in the equal treatment. Hitler gets the same brushwork as Hanuman because the man on the floor reaching for certainty, or revenge, or someone to blame, is reaching with the same desperate tenderness he reaches for the holy.</p>
                <p>The "same love" is not a moral statement. It is a confession. It admits that we adore our worst gods too. We just don't say it out loud.</p>
              </div>
            </details>
            <a class="fn-book" id="openBook" href="./books/art%20books/Calling%20All%20Gods_Artist%20Book.pdf">
              <span class="fb-l">The book is the statement too. Read it.</span>
              <span class="fb-cta">Open Calling All Gods <span class="ar" aria-hidden="true">→</span></span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

